{"title":"<span style = \"font-size: 18pt!important;\">The screening effects of naturalization costs</span>","markdown":{"yaml":{"title":"<span style = \"font-size: 18pt!important;\">The screening effects of naturalization costs</span>","subtitle":"<a href = \"https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/1ab4ya0yajco3ssh9aq96/Govind_Sirugue_2026_Screening_effect_naturalization.pdf?rlkey=fol3cha7fagit87l49vzxo0og&st=7kwcewbw&e=2&dl=0\" target=\"_blank\"><span style = \"color:#18BC9C!important;\">Latest version</span></a>, <i>with <a href = \"https://sites.google.com/view/yajnagovind/home\" target=\"_blank\"><span style = \"color:#18BC9C!important;\">Yajna Govind</span></a>, <b>R&R at Journal of Public Economics</b></i>","abstract":"<span style = \"color:#6C757D;\">We study how changing citizenship costs affect naturalization decisions and the skill composition of naturalized immigrants. We develop a theoretical framework in which benefits from citizenship are heterogeneous and costs vary with skills. We test its predictions in a quasi-experimental setting, exploiting the abolition of compulsory conscription in France as a reduction in the citizenship costs for sons of immigrants. We find that naturalization costs are higher for the low-educated, such that compulsory conscription induced positive selection on education within benefit groups. It also led to positive selection across benefit groups, which, as shown theoretically, is not guaranteed.</span>","image":"govind_sirugue_2023/out/preview.png","about":{"template":"solana","image-width":"0px","links":[{"icon":"file-earmark-text","text":"Working paper","href":"https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/1ab4ya0yajco3ssh9aq96/Govind_Sirugue_2026_Screening_effect_naturalization.pdf?rlkey=fol3cha7fagit87l49vzxo0og&st=7kwcewbw&e=2&dl=0"}]},"include-before-body":{"text":"<style>\n  .quarto-title-meta { display:none !important; visibility:hidden !important; }\n</style>\n"}},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n\n<br>\n\n```{r, echo = F}\nsource(\"c